{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness of lotteries, specifically defining a lottery as left-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (i.e., higher payoffs). In this case, Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 100% probability, meaning all outcomes are favorable and there is no risk. Lottery B offers 4000 Euros with 80% probability and 0 Euros with 20% probability. The favorable outcome (4000 Euros) occurs with 80% probability, while the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) occurs with 20%. Since the favorable outcome has a higher probability than the unfavorable one, Lottery B is left-skewed. Lottery A, with a certain outcome, is not left-skewed in the same way — it has no variability, so it lacks the asymmetry that defines skewness. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery. Thus, the reason can be applied: Lottery B is more left-skewed than A, so according to the reason, B should be preferred.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Alles aufgedeckt. Dann die letzten Zwei Wahrscheinlichkeiten verglichen. Ich finde die 3k sind schon eine ausreichend schöne Summe. Da würde ich für nur 1000€ mehr nicht die Wahrscheinlichkeit verschlechtern wollen.' This translates to: 'Everything was clarified. Then the last two probabilities were compared. I find 3000 Euros is already a sufficiently good sum. I wouldn't want to worsen the probability for just 1000 more euros.' The individual is evaluating the certainty of 3000 Euros (Lottery A) and comparing it to a risk involving a potential gain of 4000 Euros with 80% probability and a loss of 0 with 20%. The individual expresses a preference for the certain 3000 Euros, implying they are risk-averse and do not want to accept a lower expected value or reduced probability of receiving money. The justification focuses on the certainty and the perceived sufficiency of 3000 Euros, not on skewness or the distribution of probabilities in terms of left-skewness. There is no mention of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes in terms of probability weight or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — that Lottery B is more left-skewed due to a higher probability (80%) of a favorable outcome (4000 Euros) compared to a 20% chance of 0 — is not considered by the individual. Instead, the individual focuses on the certainty of 3000 Euros and expresses reluctance to accept a risk that might reduce the probability of receiving money. The justification is based on risk aversion and perceived adequacy of a certain sum, not on skewness. The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness (favorable outcomes having higher probability), which is absent from the verbal report. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The final assessment is that the reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

